       "48x48": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/9d562d2fdef1ff58e3053e0ce6aca296?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FNR-5.png"
      },
      "displayName": "Nick Ruest",
      "self": "https://fedora-repository.atlassian.net/rest/api/3/user?accountId=557058%3Afe14f014-ccfb-4882-b081-85802f8c5346",
      "timeZone": "America/Halifax"
     },
     "updated": "2015-11-03T21:37:55.725-0400"
    },
    {
     "author": {
      "accountId": "6089a48d248ef60073948d92",
      "accountType": "atlassian",
      "active": true,
      "avatarUrls": {
       "16x16": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/34cd24b6a5178b45e45b73c3c9f71e6f?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FU-0.png",
       "24x24": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/34cd24b6a5178b45e45b73c3c9f71e6f?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FU-0.png",
       "32x32": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/34cd24b6a5178b45e45b73c3c9f71e6f?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FU-0.png",
       "48x48": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/34cd24b6a5178b45e45b73c3c9f71e6f?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FU-0.png"
      },
      "displayName": "Unknown",
      "self": "https://fedora-repository.atlassian.net/rest/api/3/user?accountId=6089a48d248ef60073948d92",
      "timeZone": "America/Halifax"
     },
     "body": {
      "content": [
       {
        "content": [
         {
          "attrs": {
           "accessLevel": "",
           "id": "557058:fe14f014-ccfb-4882-b081-85802f8c5346",
           "text": "@Nick Ruest"
          },
          "type": "mention"
         },
         {
          "text": " That would be great. I keep coming back to the idea that the triplestore is, perhaps, not the place to impose access controls. At least not ",
          "type": "text"
         },
         {
          "marks": [
           {
            "type": "strong"
           }
          ],
          "text": "fine grained",
          "type": "text"
         },
         {
          "text": " access controls. Of course, if you want to expose a public sparql endpoint, that wouldn't work; unless, of course, you only put public data there.",
          "type": "text"
         }
        ],
        "type": "paragraph"
       },
       {
        "content": [
         {
          "text": "However, I wonder about the possiblities that a linked data fragments server could provide – these also seem to be more useful than full SPARQL endpoints. And in that regard, I have this idea that I've been unable to bury. And the idea is this – what if your triplestore had a basic inference engine (e.g. simple rdfs entailment) and your fedora repository is being synced to that triplestore. Then, with a slightly more sophisticated route than the current fcrepo-indexer-triplestore, you index the content to the triplestore. Then, for every fedora resource you re-query the triplestore to get all of the added inferencing. With that information (and with the information that Fedora provides w/r/t the access controls – ",
          "type": "text"
         },
         {
          "attrs": {
           "accessLevel": "",
           "id": "6089a4c12a753d0071341edb",
           "text": "@Jared Whiklo"
          },
          "type": "mention"
         },
         {
          "text": " is working on this ticket), you can pass those \"enhanced\" triples to a cassandra based database. There, you have a column family that specifically handles authZ, so you get authorization controls ",
          "type": "text"
         },
         {
          "marks": [
           {
            "type": "strong"
           }
          ],
          "text": "for every triple in the repo",
          "type": "text"
         },
         {
          "text": ". Plus, it scales like nobody's fool.",
          "type": "text"
         }
        ],
        "type": "paragraph"
       },
       {
        "content": [
         {
          "text": "I'm not entirely sure that it would work. However, in the last two months, I also haven't been able to come up with a good reason why it ",
          "type": "text"
         },
         {
          "marks": [
           {
            "type": "strong"
           }
          ],
          "text": "wouldn't",
          "type": "text"
         },
         {
          "text": " work.",
          "type": "text"
         }
        ],
        "type": "paragraph"
       }
      ],
      "type": "doc",
      "version": 1
     },
