    {
     "author": {
      "accountId": "6089a48d248ef60073948d92",
      "accountType": "atlassian",
      "active": true,
      "avatarUrls": {
       "16x16": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/34cd24b6a5178b45e45b73c3c9f71e6f?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FU-0.png",
       "24x24": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/34cd24b6a5178b45e45b73c3c9f71e6f?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FU-0.png",
       "32x32": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/34cd24b6a5178b45e45b73c3c9f71e6f?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FU-0.png",
       "48x48": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/34cd24b6a5178b45e45b73c3c9f71e6f?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FU-0.png"
      },
      "displayName": "Unknown",
      "self": "https://fedora-repository.atlassian.net/rest/api/3/user?accountId=6089a48d248ef60073948d92",
      "timeZone": "America/Halifax"
     },
     "body": "<p><a href=\"https://fedora-repository.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=557058%3Afe14f014-ccfb-4882-b081-85802f8c5346\" class=\"user-hover\" rel=\"557058:fe14f014-ccfb-4882-b081-85802f8c5346\" data-account-id=\"557058:fe14f014-ccfb-4882-b081-85802f8c5346\" accountid=\"557058:fe14f014-ccfb-4882-b081-85802f8c5346\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Nick Ruest</a> That would be great. I keep coming back to the idea that the triplestore is, perhaps, not the place to impose access controls. At least not <b>fine grained</b> access controls. Of course, if you want to expose a public sparql endpoint, that wouldn't work; unless, of course, you only put public data there.</p>\n\n<p>However, I wonder about the possiblities that a linked data fragments server could provide &#8211; these also seem to be more useful than full SPARQL endpoints. And in that regard, I have this idea that I've been unable to bury. And the idea is this &#8211; what if your triplestore had a basic inference engine (e.g. simple rdfs entailment) and your fedora repository is being synced to that triplestore. Then, with a slightly more sophisticated route than the current fcrepo-indexer-triplestore, you index the content to the triplestore. Then, for every fedora resource you re-query the triplestore to get all of the added inferencing. With that information (and with the information that Fedora provides w/r/t the access controls &#8211; <a href=\"https://fedora-repository.atlassian.net/secure/ViewProfile.jspa?accountId=6089a4c12a753d0071341edb\" class=\"user-hover\" rel=\"6089a4c12a753d0071341edb\" data-account-id=\"6089a4c12a753d0071341edb\" accountid=\"6089a4c12a753d0071341edb\" rel=\"noreferrer\">Jared Whiklo</a> is working on this ticket), you can pass those \"enhanced\" triples to a cassandra based database. There, you have a column family that specifically handles authZ, so you get authorization controls <b>for every triple in the repo</b>. Plus, it scales like nobody's fool.</p>\n\n<p>I'm not entirely sure that it would work. However, in the last two months, I also haven't been able to come up with a good reason why it <b>wouldn't</b> work.</p>",
     "created": "03/Nov/15 10:20 PM",
